    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe spec requires validating an E.164 telephone's **national-number** length against its country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting with 400 otherwise. I kept each piece of logic in the place that owns it rather than bloating the existing normalizer step:\n\n1. **`TelephoneNormalizer.hasValidNationalLength(String e164)`** \u2014 new pure helper alongside `toE164`. This class already owns canonical telephone knowledge, so the per-country digit-count rule (a `LinkedHashMap` ordered longest-code-first so `61` matches before `1`) lives here. Unknown country codes are accepted, so it only rejects a *wrong* length for a *known* country \u2014 leaving numbers from other regions to the existing 8\u201315 digit E.164 range check in `toE164`.\n\n2. **`ValidateTelephoneLength`** \u2014 a new single-responsibility pipeline step that reads the already-normalized `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` and throws when the length is wrong. I deliberately did **not** fold this into `NormalizeTelephone` (which normalizes) to avoid a catch-all.\n\n3. **`InvalidTelephoneLengthException` + handler + escalation YAML** \u2014 a checked exception mapped to 400, kept distinct from `InvalidTelephoneException` (which means \"not valid E.164 at all\") so the error semantics and message stay honest.\n\n4. **Wired the step** into `owners.POST.yml` right after `normalizeTelephone`, so it judges the canonical `+` form before uniqueness/persistence steps run.\n\nThe new test (`+61 123` \u2192 3 national digits \u2192 400) passes, and the valid `04XXXXXXXX` payload (`+61` + 9 national digits) still flows through `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
